[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272226 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133766 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aad624f-4b38-5923-afbd-1579952dbf84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269364} {:answer/id #uuid "3b956d8b-d0de-5dcf-817b-4188dc3372de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269811} {:answer/id #uuid "591adc53-138b-519d-9fb5-74c10f838635" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270055} {:answer/id #uuid "f0f1c832-b011-546e-af5d-c70d12222e25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271386} {:answer/id #uuid "fdaa9cbc-8e1e-5488-8e27-7caa05c408ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b956d8b-d0de-5dcf-817b-4188dc3372de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269811} :answer-field/id #uuid "85a148f5-fd30-5518-8877-e3dc571076f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271743}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "525bde81-7550-5f13-8e28-5c16a04ee9f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-141159" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨a,b⟩=⟨b,a⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in R$\n- $⟨a,a⟩>0$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry.\n\n- Statement II is true. For any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we have $\\overset{―}{\\alpha }=\\alpha .$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that if $a=0,$ then $⟨a,a⟩=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272227 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133766 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e483cc2-b46f-5512-a9a7-0f10ea8a953d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269394} {:answer/id #uuid "4288a7d7-b334-57bd-981d-9101054485a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269869} {:answer/id #uuid "4bf5a7d9-2cd0-5edb-8159-5892ad0bc218" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269954} {:answer/id #uuid "6d857f4b-9335-543f-9a26-45ba2569561b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270242} {:answer/id #uuid "cf364781-4d57-5ea3-a715-fb67ec1553ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d857f4b-9335-543f-9a26-45ba2569561b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270242} :answer-field/id #uuid "672798eb-6f95-52b3-882f-b5bf772f3083" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271694}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "396a82c3-ba69-5d62-8d39-94fffd303517" :question/math-academy-id "q-141160" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨ia,b⟩=i⟨a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,\\overset{―}{\\alpha }b⟩=⟨\\alpha a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C$\n- $⟨ia,a⟩\\in R$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true.  Indeed, the inner product in ${C}^{n}$ is linear in the first argument. So, a constant multiple from the first argument can be factored out.\n\n- Statement II is true. By the antilinearity of a complex inner product in the second argument and linearity in the first argument, we have \n\n$$\n⟨a,\\overset{―}{\\alpha }b⟩=\\overset{―}{(\\overset{―}{\\alpha })}⟨a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩=⟨\\alpha a,b⟩.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that $⟨a,a⟩\\in R$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}.$ Therefore, if $a\\ne 0,$ then\n\n$$\n⟨ia,a⟩=i⟨a,a⟩\\notin R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124511 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "084a0a83-5ce6-5184-beb7-8ae7b4f1a799" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74ef5d4c-f33d-597d-a2fa-aa2bd980b69e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270302} {:answer/id #uuid "91213c6e-a951-5f10-ad74-2238bac4f2f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270532} {:answer/id #uuid "945a196e-773a-511b-a5a8-a31f4dcb23f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270565} {:answer/id #uuid "956627f2-fd1b-55e8-b3da-f529dc0dbe2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270581} {:answer/id #uuid "eafe7eb0-f4b1-5c2e-a83b-f7f47ae1704d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eafe7eb0-f4b1-5c2e-a83b-f7f47ae1704d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271328} :answer-field/id #uuid "31dc903f-41ad-5ad5-b457-03d1579fa938" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f63719f4-7a1e-5b43-8302-7786de4d0619" :question/math-academy-id "q-141199" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is a solution of the equation $Ax=0?$\n\n- $\\text{Null}(A)={0}$\n- $A$ is not invertible\n- $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{4}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false. Since $v\\ne 0$ and $Av=0,$ \nthen $v\\in \\text{Null}(A)$ and, therefore, $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne {0},$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272239 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124511 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "084a0a83-5ce6-5184-beb7-8ae7b4f1a799" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "188d8c1d-5896-5f10-81c9-bd96ad0517aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269475} {:answer/id #uuid "52e6f0c7-0a62-59e3-a5cb-80e9b7f54638" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270005} {:answer/id #uuid "54208619-14dc-516d-b1aa-20ed09a0bb47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270018} {:answer/id #uuid "77353fd7-ee88-5664-aec8-e00589b061d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270324} {:answer/id #uuid "af2612fc-39ba-5012-92ba-66032676ab46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af2612fc-39ba-5012-92ba-66032676ab46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270827} :answer-field/id #uuid "94b73dce-7f8c-597e-ae8e-4e20e2b2ff1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8dad0d98-37c5-5af9-9c0a-4f0fabec2bcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-141200" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix and  $det(A)=-1?$ \n\n- $A$ is invertible\n- $\\text{Null}(A)={0}$\n- $\\text{nullity}(A)=6$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{6} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=6=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,   a matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$ Since here we have $det(A)=-1\\ne 0,$ our matrix $A$ must be  invertible.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, by the invertible matrix theorem\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\text{Null}(A)={0}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is invertible,  it must be $\\text{nullity}(A)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272240 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083303 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1894719c-d398-515d-9a7b-6af74727106c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Improper Integrals of the Second Kind"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26a4ad32-a873-582b-96ae-ed02f26f7e8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269609} {:answer/id #uuid "6514b6a0-1a44-54a4-a5cf-1f9f398648a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270169} {:answer/id #uuid "668a240d-1631-510d-a9b4-c9d7bbdd0f99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270179} {:answer/id #uuid "79ea0306-d45d-5d31-b36c-d44f29d74ce1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270348} {:answer/id #uuid "fd2dadc1-bee4-5e05-a544-04e4b92d8545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271484}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26a4ad32-a873-582b-96ae-ed02f26f7e8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269609} :answer-field/id #uuid "c371ee98-f872-5961-b48b-e16e995f5c79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6a9082ef-b5c5-508a-ad30-c16427769a4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-141209" :question/problem "Which of the following is an improper integral of the second kind?\n\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{3}-8}\\,$  II. ${\\int }_{-2}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{3}+8}\\,$  III. ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{3}}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I is not an improper integral since it has finite limits and the function under the integral is finite at every point $x\\in [0,1].$\n\n- Integral II is an improper integral of the second kind since it has finite limits, and \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to (-2)^{+}.\n$$\n\n- Integral III is an improper integral over an *infinite* domain since its lower limit is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272241 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083303 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1894719c-d398-515d-9a7b-6af74727106c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Improper Integrals of the Second Kind"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32879a09-f2f5-5307-852d-bdcc80ac28fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269732} {:answer/id #uuid "5193b80b-aad5-55dd-8026-5fcce1aaa215" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269999} {:answer/id #uuid "7321fe4c-6365-5e6c-9321-5a1e32c712c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270288} {:answer/id #uuid "e05f83bc-9cbf-50fa-8e83-a22bd0a5bf8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271240} {:answer/id #uuid "efacb9a9-087a-548a-a494-9ae0ca07c85a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e05f83bc-9cbf-50fa-8e83-a22bd0a5bf8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271240} :answer-field/id #uuid "b230196c-4db7-51b1-b127-98b23dc5d317" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5c6c3c56-7a5d-5137-ba4b-48f3b706f731" :question/math-academy-id "q-141212" :question/problem "Which of the following is an improper integral of the second kind?\n\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{x}-1}\\,$  II. ${\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{1-\\sqrt{x}}\\,$  III. ${\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{x-1}}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I is an improper integral over an *infinite* domain since its upper limit is infinite.\n\n- Integral II is not an improper integral since it has finite limits and the function under the integral is finite at every point $x\\in [2,4].$\n\n- Integral III is an improper integral of the second kind since it has finite limits, and \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {1}^{+}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272242 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083303 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1894719c-d398-515d-9a7b-6af74727106c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Improper Integrals of the Second Kind"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e0ef079-5f06-5e7c-adef-068bae5538ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269519} {:answer/id #uuid "357e3f3b-92ad-5333-b803-0f7d4d33b7d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269758} {:answer/id #uuid "55026d12-8574-5fbe-8c07-f7e5bd6d4ff3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270025} {:answer/id #uuid "cfc10ea1-5dc4-5c43-8e23-bce8bb264142" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271090} {:answer/id #uuid "d7b0eda8-710f-5ddd-b5e0-4b4ab8b7f985" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "357e3f3b-92ad-5333-b803-0f7d4d33b7d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269758} :answer-field/id #uuid "57dc7a29-f195-55f8-9696-8efd8402e367" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c19bd8b-6180-555e-84ee-4d1df41e83f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-141218" :question/problem "Which of the following is an improper integral of the second kind?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\,$  II. ${\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\infty }\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\,$  III. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I is an improper integral of the second kind since it has finite limits, and \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\to -\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {0}^{-}.\n$$\n\n- Integral II is an improper integral over an *infinite* domain since its upper limit is infinite.\n\n- Integral III is not an improper integral since it has finite limits and the function under the integral is finite at every point $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272247 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092327 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eab94cb-5a5a-5915-ab95-963cb4c63817" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Closure Properties of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ce55ae1-51e7-5113-8b9b-9cc54aa7d0de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269380} {:answer/id #uuid "46da9874-30bc-583c-a863-5aae9560a9cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269913} {:answer/id #uuid "59a1700f-ccf6-5e35-9e43-a7880fc832ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270065} {:answer/id #uuid "75df7d2e-780f-56cd-92ad-5720fda4a706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270310} {:answer/id #uuid "c456bd0a-afad-5d98-8f69-257b595ed130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ce55ae1-51e7-5113-8b9b-9cc54aa7d0de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269380} :answer-field/id #uuid "903f2292-1c52-539d-84aa-b1a6a93b0960" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6faa06e9-6d50-5778-8c1a-50c6b3712803" :question/math-academy-id "q-141234" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n- $(x)+(-{x}^{3}-1)$\n- $(2{x}^{4}-3)÷({x}^{2})$\n- $({x}^{2})-(2{x}^{5}+4x)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is a polynomial. It's a sum of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under addition.\n\n- Expression II is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{4}-3}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\n- Expression III gives a polynomial. It's a difference between two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under subtraction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272248 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092327 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eab94cb-5a5a-5915-ab95-963cb4c63817" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Closure Properties of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "023f4ad7-4e27-58df-b9cf-7d7dff9ec1d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269287} {:answer/id #uuid "67f707f9-a11f-5cd5-b66d-da214b7f66c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270192} {:answer/id #uuid "d00b4f12-2503-53b5-ad0e-43dad5b4ff65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271096} {:answer/id #uuid "d9e53e33-d114-5a0d-8f9d-97c29350e249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of these listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271178} {:answer/id #uuid "fbc39fd6-b0b1-5463-86d7-17f95b8035dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271475}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d00b4f12-2503-53b5-ad0e-43dad5b4ff65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271096} :answer-field/id #uuid "2352a387-0098-58a2-9edc-386e6c666edd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271575}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a6242aae-9378-55e8-b137-ddfe7f38d97f" :question/math-academy-id "q-141236" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n- $(4{x}^{6}-2)÷(2x)$\n- $(7x+1)\\cdot (2{x}^{3}+3)$\n- $({x}^{4}-3{x}^{2}+1)÷({x}^{2})$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{x}^{6}-2}{2x} & =\\frac{4{x}^{6}}{2x}-\\frac{2}{2x} \\\\  & =2{x}^{5}-\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\n- Expression II gives a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\n- Expression III is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{4}-3{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-3+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272249 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092327 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eab94cb-5a5a-5915-ab95-963cb4c63817" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Closure Properties of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "576103fa-ed39-5cc1-b3dd-aa1c41c6e6b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270040} {:answer/id #uuid "850b62e1-b775-571c-9058-04cc09e464b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270419} {:answer/id #uuid "b9d1b938-e87f-5462-aecb-d2ec3dd3795d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270910} {:answer/id #uuid "d48c0194-9812-5494-b598-95ff7ec91143" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271137} {:answer/id #uuid "d736a809-e9dd-5b1c-8eab-1c5a18819085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "850b62e1-b775-571c-9058-04cc09e464b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270419} :answer-field/id #uuid "86c7e2f7-2db6-5d5d-9fb1-1b41e12897f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271746}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2a3f0c75-73fd-5349-840a-8899dcbd367a" :question/math-academy-id "q-141240" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n- $(4{x}^{6}-x)+(3{x}^{5})$\n- $({x}^{3}-1)\\cdot (3-{x}^{2})$\n- $({x}^{3}-4{x}^{2})÷(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is a polynomial. It's a sum of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under addition.\n\n- Expression II is a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\n- Expression III is a polynomial. Even though polynomials are *not* closed under division, the quotient of these two polynomials results in a polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{3}-4{x}^{2})÷(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x({x}^{2}-4x)}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x}({x}^{2}-4x)}{\\cancel{x}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272250 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092327 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eab94cb-5a5a-5915-ab95-963cb4c63817" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Closure Properties of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e78141c-5b1b-5383-9436-dd7961708aa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269398} {:answer/id #uuid "377e209c-dca0-560e-b0e9-3dd954d6f5d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269771} {:answer/id #uuid "b9af0254-5aa7-512b-b479-10e85a3c90b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270908} {:answer/id #uuid "eba4ea75-16bf-56cf-b877-0a5e9c421d89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of these listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271340} {:answer/id #uuid "f61c3215-545e-5c9e-9d54-4f213478cea5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e78141c-5b1b-5383-9436-dd7961708aa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269398} :answer-field/id #uuid "26ded575-1ef1-5611-9129-6853eed9845c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271580}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "78e46443-6b0b-52be-80af-856b64b460d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-141245" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n- $(3{x}^{3}-2x)÷({x}^{3})$\n- $(10{x}^{6}-4{x}^{3})÷(2{x}^{3})$\n- $(2x-1)\\cdot ({x}^{3}+6x)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{3}-2x}{{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =3-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\n- Expression II is a polynomial. Even though polynomials are *not* closed under division, the quotient of these two polynomials results in a polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(10{x}^{6}-4{x}^{3})÷(2{x}^{3}) & =\\frac{10{x}^{6}-4{x}^{3}}{2{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{3}(5{x}^{3}-2)}{2{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{2{x}^{3}}(5{x}^{3}-2)}{\\cancel{2{x}^{3}}} \\\\  & =5{x}^{3}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Expression III is a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272287 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124521 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77214-796e-558f-a364-00ae3e25cc13" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30c30111-8967-5105-a0b6-6a23513210c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269710} {:answer/id #uuid "631335b6-7ef6-59ce-8e7e-749eea9ebc94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270145} {:answer/id #uuid "668c0601-7469-5a85-8564-3965eb0e1791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270180} {:answer/id #uuid "bf4f4ba1-902f-5cdd-9b4c-bb432b2696b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270962} {:answer/id #uuid "dfabd0e7-90be-5aeb-9da3-657d3cdb0121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf4f4ba1-902f-5cdd-9b4c-bb432b2696b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270962} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d9f08da-5269-5b1f-bbcd-8c03985365d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c894da3b-5f0a-59a9-8060-52795d04ecee" :question/math-academy-id "q-141460" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $T:{R}^{7}\\to {R}^{7}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and $T(x)=v$ has no solution for some vector $v?$ \n\n- $T$ is not invertible\n- $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$\n- $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 7$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{7}$ into ${R}^{7},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{7} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=7 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $T(x)=v$ has no solution, we have that $v\\notin \\text{Im}(T),$ hence $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{7}.$ According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{7},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272288 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124521 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77214-796e-558f-a364-00ae3e25cc13" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "208d1741-9b10-5838-b5d0-cf5b8af2cbc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269544} {:answer/id #uuid "6e85d1f1-1d07-59dc-a62c-55b15d9603cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270250} {:answer/id #uuid "70ac0a62-886f-57c1-aea0-e66bd7901f22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270268} {:answer/id #uuid "9754293a-effe-5066-a8b6-e33c8c81be55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270596} {:answer/id #uuid "dffd9eb1-554e-5064-8f37-aa14a874c3f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e85d1f1-1d07-59dc-a62c-55b15d9603cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270250} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cdc4b03-2ed5-57f1-96a3-caaa4804b205" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271591}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2fc1f275-1b92-5d39-91a9-c77fc478abd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-141461" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $T:{R}^{4}\\to {R}^{4}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and $T$ is a bijection? \n\n- $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$\n- $\\text{rank}(T)=4$\n- $T(v)=0$ for some $v\\ne 0$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{4}$ into ${R}^{4},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=4 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)=4.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Ker}(T)={0},$ which means there is no non-zero vector $v$ such that $T(v)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272291 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a601e-220a-54a3-a64b-1e554bc3d79f" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "429117a3-6e54-516f-90dc-8b3c46852d73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269870} {:answer/id #uuid "540ff262-b6d8-55d6-bdff-7a8f7374caf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270017} {:answer/id #uuid "953e5319-0e76-5d91-be9e-97acbe17adc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270577} {:answer/id #uuid "c637029f-ce11-54ac-92f8-b41a82831cf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271015} {:answer/id #uuid "f27f32f7-f9d9-50bf-b772-90b1d8509143" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f27f32f7-f9d9-50bf-b772-90b1d8509143" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271401} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f70c062-2c20-514b-a050-6ed7cd19ecf6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "496f9381-a057-5d31-a929-726bf731d70b" :question/math-academy-id "q-141495" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\in {R}^{3}.$  Consider the following statement:\n\n*If $2T({v}_{1})-3T({v}_{2})=0$ and ${v}_{1}\\ne k{v}_{2}$ for all $k\\in R$ then $\\text{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$\n- $\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3}$\n- $T$ is invertible" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $2T({v}_{1})-3T({v}_{2})=0$ and $T$ is a linear transformation, we have $T(2{v}_{1}-3{v}_{2})=0$ which means $2{v}_{1}-3{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$   Since ${v}_{1}\\ne k{v}_{2}$,  we must have $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the transformation $T$ is not a bijection. Since $T$ is not a bijection, then by the invertible matrix theorem, $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3},$ the corresponding matrix $T$ can't be invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272301 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a601e-220a-54a3-a64b-1e554bc3d79f" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09aa63a2-fcd6-59c7-b8d5-c7ae94cea07b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269357} {:answer/id #uuid "222c1fca-f9be-5ece-b1ab-40086be18680" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269563} {:answer/id #uuid "a1454b89-eae8-5cc9-b299-541dbb47ae01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270698} {:answer/id #uuid "cebbe3cb-717f-56f5-b65c-230ba3342707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271082} {:answer/id #uuid "f48fdfad-2d33-560f-be43-a5d4c13ef17b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271412}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "222c1fca-f9be-5ece-b1ab-40086be18680" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269563} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddf7ad5e-33c5-5362-84f5-c267e5402f28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c4507db3-2027-5030-88fd-955381171b17" :question/math-academy-id "q-141569" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{4}\\to {R}^{4}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If $\\text{nullity}(T)=4,$ then $\\text{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{4}$\n- $T$ is not invertible\n- $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 4$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{4}$ into ${R}^{4},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=4 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0,$ the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{4}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0,$ the invertible matrix theorem implies that $T$ is not invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272302 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a601e-220a-54a3-a64b-1e554bc3d79f" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30b3cb5a-341a-59e5-a715-d12977c5bed1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269708} {:answer/id #uuid "5fe66b4f-fc34-5672-a3db-4734e66a55a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270116} {:answer/id #uuid "96a19ab7-cc61-5293-86a8-bafa6581e8e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270594} {:answer/id #uuid "cb05382b-ecd7-547d-89c0-4aef033b29c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271053} {:answer/id #uuid "da7eed7b-ee2d-5936-b766-061ccc59fa6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96a19ab7-cc61-5293-86a8-bafa6581e8e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270594} :answer-field/id #uuid "32ad95d2-baf4-5b0d-81cc-ba23c5a39a70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2805dfb0-8d9a-5775-8051-a005b74b03a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-141584" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{6}\\to {R}^{6}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If ${v}_{1}\\ne {v}_{2}$ vectors such that $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})$, then $\\text{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$\n- $\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{6}$\n- $T$ is not a bijection" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{6}$ into ${R}^{6},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{6} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=6 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$\n\nSince $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\nT({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})\\,⟹\\,T({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since ${v}_{1}-{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and ${v}_{1}\\ne {v}_{2},$ we must have that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible. Since $T$ is not invertible, by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have that $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{6}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{6},$ the invertible matrix theorem implies that the transformation $T$ is not a bijection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272306 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064312 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bd5c660-b216-59b4-b2bb-df83ef864b2a" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Concave Up and Concave Down Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "093de82c-3c10-509e-a115-10c02890e9d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269353} {:answer/id #uuid "138068fd-1aed-52e6-ac5f-add922c4dca9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269443} {:answer/id #uuid "3638d5d5-8aa0-56ce-b0f5-e70107346880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269762} {:answer/id #uuid "f79af8a6-d754-5246-a9ad-95b33a49ce2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271441} {:answer/id #uuid "fdae7495-8e53-5c68-9f1d-812a9ee8c272" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fdae7495-8e53-5c68-9f1d-812a9ee8c272" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271490} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd64f202-1cd1-5be2-bcac-f3c8e7039c15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271947}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1bb3b4f0-a153-5b1f-93aa-f136a6132e95" :question/math-academy-id "q-141751" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9d5ea7e13fa03ac553893aa78aa7ee32ac7b67f2eb19dff32228d53bf2010aa2.png)\n\nWhich of the curves above are concave down?" :question/worked-solution "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOnly graphs I and III curve downward (like a \"frown\") among the given graphs."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272307 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064312 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bd5c660-b216-59b4-b2bb-df83ef864b2a" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Concave Up and Concave Down Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5177ea3a-9272-590f-8f16-d605b524db47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269998} {:answer/id #uuid "5c3a782a-c7a0-5f39-9b30-7e1f9ae49e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270090} {:answer/id #uuid "7cc2623b-ee3b-5cc2-863d-41dcce18a077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270363} {:answer/id #uuid "a640d8d9-7217-514b-aa3a-b01bab9dbd46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270757} {:answer/id #uuid "be7733e3-82d9-51c4-9a92-5cf1de7b00b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270951}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cc2623b-ee3b-5cc2-863d-41dcce18a077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270363} :answer-field/id #uuid "6372289f-4413-56d4-a466-f00fa976e952" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271685}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "13227862-4183-5d5b-b484-99ef3e29fd6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-141754" :question/problem "![](images/b2/b2b904027ec6319fe5857f81089469568322d148e49cdf31bb20c07f1f31daf4.png)\n\nWhich of the curves above are concave down?" :question/worked-solution "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nAll graphs I, II, and III curve downward (like a \"frown\") among the given graphs."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272338 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124511 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "084a0a83-5ce6-5184-beb7-8ae7b4f1a799" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21c27800-0300-5249-b4a0-b6e008f8e83d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269558} {:answer/id #uuid "44d71b0a-4454-5106-ad0c-ed629eabb1a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269900} {:answer/id #uuid "708d14c2-579e-5e65-9cd2-447b05f510fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270265} {:answer/id #uuid "a29c9c7d-1f79-5b6d-b1d6-1b0537c578cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270720} {:answer/id #uuid "f7830376-f651-56ea-b56d-c7df40cee1ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44d71b0a-4454-5106-ad0c-ed629eabb1a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269900} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3f46adb-5d16-505a-be52-2235f2f08ca3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0c320bbb-0961-56b0-99e4-beaa75aa8f10" :question/math-academy-id "q-142358" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)={[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R}?\n$$\n\n- $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=0$\n- $A$ is  invertible\n- $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{5}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)={{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=2\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0,$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272347 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124511 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "084a0a83-5ce6-5184-beb7-8ae7b4f1a799" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "302eb5c9-529d-52e1-a2c8-d1933af5ef1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269702} {:answer/id #uuid "3248b20a-91c4-5f7a-8779-ea6de359ff74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269724} {:answer/id #uuid "50abda73-b0d4-5a29-bbed-94088d819a8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269993} {:answer/id #uuid "81cab381-305e-5227-96c9-4247414173f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270391} {:answer/id #uuid "aa256175-82e0-56ad-9450-2d16ed6ca6ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "302eb5c9-529d-52e1-a2c8-d1933af5ef1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269702} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecf48405-f1ff-5682-8eff-4a48eebe4c60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271926}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4927fd87-c83e-52d3-b9bb-877110a8c514" :question/math-academy-id "q-142474" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)={[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R}?\n$$\n\n- $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=1$\n- $A$ is invertible\n- $\\text{rank}(A)<4$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)={{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=2.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0,$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 4,$ or equivalently $\\text{rank}(A)<4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272348 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124629 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb90e5b9-03fe-51cf-bc16-a293befacebc" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ccbab42-afa2-5f66-ac0e-c308c66e8202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269668} {:answer/id #uuid "82c7cc8b-3d11-56b8-a528-bbb1a33f1d5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270399} {:answer/id #uuid "952d8149-19cf-533c-af96-872b8df55506" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270576} {:answer/id #uuid "d8826fb6-eeca-5b0d-8d8b-f2147e992c35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271170} {:answer/id #uuid "e2d12440-aa2a-5690-b9a2-07e79a593ee7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2d12440-aa2a-5690-b9a2-07e79a593ee7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271262} :answer-field/id #uuid "10bb68a7-51a0-5f0c-b2b1-a21badc3475a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271534}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "795129ab-7204-5692-a97c-e32525213e2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-142475" :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then  $\\text{__________}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $A$ is invertible\n- $\\text{rank}(A)<n$\n- $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $A$ is invertible.* $\\,✓$ \n\nIndeed, according to the invertible matrix theorem, \n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A).$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $\\text{rank}(A)<n.$* $\\,\\times $ \n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{rank}(A)=n.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n}.$*  $\\,✓$ \n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272349 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124629 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb90e5b9-03fe-51cf-bc16-a293befacebc" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0895ab25-c3a3-5de5-8c7f-b29240d396fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269347} {:answer/id #uuid "70dbe989-e5af-54e0-b64e-b2b8f149ec2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270271} {:answer/id #uuid "8abda789-acc5-5411-8952-794fc73da646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270468} {:answer/id #uuid "a4e8d21b-4e62-5248-a3d7-8afaad5786f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270745} {:answer/id #uuid "be53ddb2-3789-59ea-8a22-87a097d55924" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270949}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0895ab25-c3a3-5de5-8c7f-b29240d396fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269347} :answer-field/id #uuid "afe26b88-241f-530c-8ccf-982cdb7e7d4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7036dc73-9ca4-59f2-b983-11eaacfb5a4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-142476" :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $8\\times 8$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then $\\text{__________}$ .*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $det(A)=0$\n- $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{16}$\n- $A$ has $8$ pivot columns" :question/worked-solution "If $A$ is an $8\\times 8$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{8} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=8=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $8\\times 8$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then $det(A)=0$* $\\,\\times $  \n\nSince the matrix $A$ is invertible, it must be  $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $8\\times 8$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then  $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{16}.$*  $\\,\\times $\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem,  we have $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{8}.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $8\\times 8$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then $A$ has $8$ pivot columns.* $\\,✓$ \n\nSince $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=8,$ which means that $A$ has exactly $8$ pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272354 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184172 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e145cb2-806a-579d-8299-8a91a6feaad1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem and the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14c41b95-9596-5622-8c50-ab125bfd2d1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269450} {:answer/id #uuid "219e0837-40ab-56ac-83a2-e830963ec4b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269555} {:answer/id #uuid "31ab7072-4441-5672-aae6-91ba5c541bbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269716} {:answer/id #uuid "36aebee8-74d8-57f5-bd26-af2dbc3f65cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269765} {:answer/id #uuid "a3580b5c-8adc-5496-932c-814534e0f45c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36aebee8-74d8-57f5-bd26-af2dbc3f65cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269765} :answer-field/id #uuid "d78fac35-412b-5106-bc11-6026065b218b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271890}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a4ded73-dd50-5431-9dd3-a7f4831d94c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-142544" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix which has only $3$ non-pivot column?\n\n- $A$ is  **not** invertible\n- $\\text{Col}(A)$ is a $4$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{7}.$\n- $\\text{nullity}(A)=1$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{7} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=7=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $A$ has $n=7$ columns and $3$ of them is a non-pivot one, it follows that    \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Col}(A))=7-3=4\\ne 7.\n$$\n\n Thus,  according to the invertible matrix theorem $A$ is  **not** an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement II is true.  Indeed, since $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=\\text{rank}(A)=4$,  then $\\text{Col}(A)$ is a $4$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{7}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $n=7$, according to  the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 4+\\text{nullity}(A) & =7 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =7-4 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275088 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096691 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c015dcaa-2155-5d80-985b-21924de92b28" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Equalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ca6964b-f001-50a6-962e-37568669ccd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272499} {:answer/id #uuid "38adbd90-9f47-5cfa-b9c1-7dcb290c0580" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272883} {:answer/id #uuid "759af798-3374-5082-9e33-2c081c92dd51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273389} {:answer/id #uuid "c73c83bf-34b1-563e-83a1-c83796722f15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274119} {:answer/id #uuid "fa69b46f-7365-5071-aefe-c9610d1635b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c73c83bf-34b1-563e-83a1-c83796722f15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274119} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c232438-e4d9-548e-93c1-3bdeab4b5cac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6378e369-5031-5af4-8366-576c75d82fa3" :question/math-academy-id "q-143001" :question/problem "![](images/67/67706db50684aef52269d20065caa21e4301834835e10e1fd3e456646ae2b32e.png)\n\nIn figure $ABCDEF$ shown above, which of the following are equal to the vector $\\overset{\\to }{DF}?$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{DC}+\\overset{\\to }{CB}+\\overset{\\to }{BF}$\n- $\\overset{\\to }{FB}-\\overset{\\to }{DB}$\n- $\\overset{\\to }{DE}+\\overset{\\to }{EF}$" :question/worked-solution "- For the $1$st expression, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DC}+\\overset{\\to }{CB}+\\overset{\\to }{BF} & =(\\overset{\\to }{DC}+\\overset{\\to }{CB})+\\overset{\\to }{BF} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{DB}+\\overset{\\to }{BF} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{DF}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the $2$nd expression, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{FB}-\\overset{\\to }{DB} & =\\overset{\\to }{FB}+\\overset{\\to }{BD} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{FD} \\\\  & \\ne \\overset{\\to }{DF}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the $3$rd expression, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE}+\\overset{\\to }{EF} & =\\overset{\\to }{DF}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275089 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096691 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c015dcaa-2155-5d80-985b-21924de92b28" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Equalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "039f4640-df8c-5f58-8473-a42a9d3b80e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272417} {:answer/id #uuid "4dffa4f0-5259-5130-adb8-cb6e99c26fb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273042} {:answer/id #uuid "5a2716f3-ecca-55e3-979f-f0158825f290" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273160} {:answer/id #uuid "c042c4d0-435e-5e30-98e5-20c5ed9387ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274051} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb3ddd5-6976-5815-aaaa-8715e648bc5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c042c4d0-435e-5e30-98e5-20c5ed9387ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274051} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1ed2a0e-62c9-5160-8b40-423a8e0dfc90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275030}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "32cdac5a-ca17-59c1-8641-8b3b47e09944" :question/math-academy-id "q-143006" :question/problem "![](images/15/15f4bd7f8ecf8f61ce84858e33cdfa468b0de360fac19b0713b6b2dbc4af8d2e.png)\n\nIn figure $ABCDEF$ shown above, which of the following equalities are true?\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AB}-\\overset{\\to }{AF}=\\overset{\\to }{BF}$\n- $\\overset{\\to }{DE}+\\overset{\\to }{EA}-\\overset{\\to }{BA}=\\overset{\\to }{DB}$\n- $\\overset{\\to }{BE}-\\overset{\\to }{DE}-\\overset{\\to }{CD}=\\overset{\\to }{CB}$" :question/worked-solution "- Equality I is false. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}-\\overset{\\to }{AF} & =\\overset{\\to }{AB}+\\overset{\\to }{FA} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{FB} \\\\  & \\ne \\overset{\\to }{BF}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equality II is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE}+\\overset{\\to }{EA}-\\overset{\\to }{BA} & =\\overset{\\to }{DE}+(\\overset{\\to }{EA}+\\overset{\\to }{AB}) \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{DE}+\\overset{\\to }{EB} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{DB}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equality III is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{BE}-\\overset{\\to }{DE}-\\overset{\\to }{CD} & =(\\overset{\\to }{BE}+\\overset{\\to }{ED})+\\overset{\\to }{DC} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{BD}+\\overset{\\to }{DC} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{BC} \\\\  & \\ne \\overset{\\to }{CB}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275114 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162153 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "196e7d31-5af8-5c82-b2bd-0cf1ed7415e2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent Given the Joint PMF Only"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "025d6b48-01e5-5f01-8d65-c71247ac2c9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272402} {:answer/id #uuid "33299f41-5d8f-547d-ad5c-48ed2d2cc7f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272842} {:answer/id #uuid "4db325db-0d80-5c94-8d47-63cd60da22e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273039} {:answer/id #uuid "b708c468-1a5f-5884-843d-d4de1dfc18a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273974} {:answer/id #uuid "dbc201f5-22fa-5ca2-81f0-aa15cf93f039" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274314}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b708c468-1a5f-5884-843d-d4de1dfc18a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273974} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bc06cb9-a10b-5ac4-80d0-be2806f417c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a347e7c2-3db8-5058-926f-eeec20d91744" :question/math-academy-id "q-143331" :question/problem "The joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.6$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.15$ | $0.05$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P(X=2,Y=1)=P(X=2)\\cdot P(Y=1)$\n- $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n- $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.6$ | $0.2$ | $0.8$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.15$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.75$ | $0.25$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=2,Y=1)=f(2,1)=0.15.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=2)\\cdot P(Y=1)={f}_{X}(2)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1)=0.2\\cdot 0.75=0.15.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & =0.6=0.8\\cdot 0.75={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,2) & =0.2=0.8\\cdot 0.25={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & ✓ \\\\ f(2,2) & =0.05=0.2\\cdot 0.25={f}_{X}(2)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275115 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162153 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "196e7d31-5af8-5c82-b2bd-0cf1ed7415e2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent Given the Joint PMF Only"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cb511a6-9e3f-5fab-80ba-b266dbff4317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272500} {:answer/id #uuid "0f80c1c4-b8b0-5d20-8f58-ee939af16ade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272524} {:answer/id #uuid "8e4845ad-be2d-5904-9060-d4ff681ee8d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273616} {:answer/id #uuid "baf4e5bc-43d2-59fe-b996-7b9f861daebb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274009} {:answer/id #uuid "fda572d8-a7cb-50d0-afbf-d1498df635a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0cb511a6-9e3f-5fab-80ba-b266dbff4317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272500} :answer-field/id #uuid "a47cbf0e-0dfc-5b44-a81b-f3239de9f23e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274922}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e48e8cd-fab9-56d8-9673-3c5737feeff5" :question/math-academy-id "q-143333" :question/problem "The joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P(X=2,Y=2)=P(X=2)\\cdot P(Y=2)$\n- $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n- $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.5$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ | $0.5$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=2,Y=2)=f(2,2)=0.4.$ However, \n\n$$\nP(X=2)\\cdot P(Y=2)={f}_{X}(2)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2)=0.5\\cdot 0.6=0.3.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nP(X=2,Y=2)\\ne P(X=2)\\cdot P(Y=2),\n$$\n\nand $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275116 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162153 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "196e7d31-5af8-5c82-b2bd-0cf1ed7415e2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent Given the Joint PMF Only"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0546ac24-d2f1-5953-9176-6d38e1f738e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272437} {:answer/id #uuid "36567941-4a76-538e-b399-81b828902e4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272866} {:answer/id #uuid "adcc90b9-7b62-57f4-b547-8bcf79edd9d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273886} {:answer/id #uuid "d1b0d5ca-bc00-5e71-b084-1b238b1ba8f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274219} {:answer/id #uuid "f2536e7d-d2f7-5f66-9627-aed4d7e02129" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274528}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36567941-4a76-538e-b399-81b828902e4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272866} :answer-field/id #uuid "96723ab9-6095-5228-8b7f-012e24dab80f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "16b71f57-df8c-5727-afc2-cdadae9185c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-143334" :question/problem "The joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.6$ | $0.15$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P(X=0,Y=3)=P(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=3)$\n- $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n- $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.15$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.6$ | $0.15$ | $0.75$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.7$ | $0.3$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=0,Y=3)=f(0,3)=0.15.$ However, \n\n$$\nP(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=3)={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(3)=0.25\\cdot 0.3=0.075.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nP(X=0,Y=3)\\ne P(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=3),\n$$\n\nand $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275117 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162153 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "196e7d31-5af8-5c82-b2bd-0cf1ed7415e2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent Given the Joint PMF Only"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29a8f8d7-4d6f-5a08-8231-327845ba4d85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272758} {:answer/id #uuid "610da49c-8865-5852-b87b-a49c0a223ca9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273227} {:answer/id #uuid "87a51537-6779-5081-93a5-5585b9685064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273549} {:answer/id #uuid "cef4a47c-100b-5941-af4b-e9103ef31cba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274188} {:answer/id #uuid "f8ec2394-8af4-54b2-b440-029010d2bb5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274568}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29a8f8d7-4d6f-5a08-8231-327845ba4d85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272758} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a58c3f2-6a19-560b-9e61-46106b8cc5d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274737}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "743eb09d-bf29-57ce-b05b-46da6dccd3f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-143338" :question/problem "The joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.16$ | $0.01$ | $0.03$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.64$ | $0.09$ | $0.07$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P(X=3,Y=2)=P(X=3)\\cdot P(Y=2)$\n- $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n- $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=6$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.16$ | $0.01$ | $0.03$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.64$ | $0.09$ | $0.07$ | $0.8$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.8$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=3,Y=2)=f(3,2)=0.64.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=3)\\cdot P(Y=2)={f}_{X}(3)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2)=0.8\\cdot 0.8=0.64.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Checking some possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,2) & =0.16=0.2\\cdot 0.8={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,4) & =0.01\\ne 0.2\\cdot 0.1={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(4) & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275118 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162153 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "196e7d31-5af8-5c82-b2bd-0cf1ed7415e2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent Given the Joint PMF Only"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21bb620a-f093-5caa-b1a9-20fc5f8cfee9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272687} {:answer/id #uuid "80870de8-5e3b-5b1b-9028-7729c2eaea11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273489} {:answer/id #uuid "8c12813a-38d4-579d-a916-788bd5895610" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273595} {:answer/id #uuid "91d2a50b-e57c-5a57-b276-1c5c736e21b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273655} {:answer/id #uuid "e56b3679-73df-5047-b9c4-e444f35afcad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274413}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c12813a-38d4-579d-a916-788bd5895610" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273595} :answer-field/id #uuid "1956cca5-6b5f-5c1b-a8aa-e051fe415e86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274681}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d571385e-cc3b-5950-8471-24ba60f93b53" :question/math-academy-id "q-143435" :question/problem "The joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.6$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.25$ | $0.05$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P(X=1,Y=1)=P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)$\n- $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n- $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.6$ | $0.7$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.25$ | $0.05$ | $0.3$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.35$ | $0.65$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=1,Y=1)=f(1,1)=0.05.$ However, \n\n$$\nP(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1)=0.3\\cdot 0.65=0.195.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nP(X=1,Y=1)\\ne P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1),\n$$\n\nand $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275119 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184172 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e145cb2-806a-579d-8299-8a91a6feaad1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem and the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54e429d6-5947-5ad4-8a8e-1b4250c00d74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273111} {:answer/id #uuid "5c3fa507-d42c-5103-9bce-8c5f53c501b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273180} {:answer/id #uuid "e1c0cc8f-fdd7-579a-8085-29cf09cbcaa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274381} {:answer/id #uuid "f79576de-3c96-5d9f-9017-a24e98f90c5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274559} {:answer/id #uuid "fe165258-8e9f-54a9-8b34-94b679b7bcec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe165258-8e9f-54a9-8b34-94b679b7bcec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274621} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b6204e6-2ff1-5255-b86a-87eaf2ede716" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5342a760-4f6b-57f7-989c-35eadd9600b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-143533" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and  $\\text{Null}(A)={[\\begin{aligned}2a \\\\ a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,a,b\\in R}?$ \n\n- $\\text{rank}(A)=3$\n- $A$ is invertible\n- $det(A)=0$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=3=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$  \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)={[\\begin{aligned}2a \\\\ a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,a,b\\in R}=\\text{Span}{\\,[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\n \nwe have that $\\text{nullity}(A)=2.$\n\nNow,  for the matrix $A,$ the number of columns is $n=3,$ so according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =3 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{nullity}(A)=2\\ne 0,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ can't be an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $A$ is not invertible we must have   $det(A)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275134 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161979 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c2da07e-23b5-58d0-aeef-14e21b329368" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46c4360e-11aa-50a5-8dca-4dea8a8fdd3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272992} {:answer/id #uuid "892b0517-6002-5679-8747-3e18957a3c43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273568} {:answer/id #uuid "c1d73e2e-3487-5a60-a79c-f7913c20e904" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274069} {:answer/id #uuid "ca570e3b-a84a-5db7-a59c-54a705149244" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274144} {:answer/id #uuid "d1f2b8dc-2166-5acf-96d8-0e209e971eb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274221}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "892b0517-6002-5679-8747-3e18957a3c43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273568} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8185736-7186-5fec-abfc-3ab0eb208611" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275070}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2475e8ae-4517-52f5-bd41-fe3d91352945" :question/math-academy-id "q-143680" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 11 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -9 \\\\ 11 & -9 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $Q$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275135 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162024 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bcd0ca26-3f22-533f-9d08-1533eeddbc79" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Properties of Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08aa5e40-f907-50f4-951b-c71140a5352e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272462} {:answer/id #uuid "3a8077df-ab1d-5da4-88d8-80f362bafcad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272895} {:answer/id #uuid "9a5e2eb0-f74e-552b-b366-11725709bbb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273721} {:answer/id #uuid "9bf1cae4-760d-5c26-a55c-ccba7d6302c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273739} {:answer/id #uuid "b3e1a958-b3c4-543b-af2a-389cf376a09a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273946}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a5e2eb0-f74e-552b-b366-11725709bbb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273721} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ad96634-e2da-5afd-bd87-58989b7b67fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "df88ef9d-a81d-55a1-b088-252f805046b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-143685" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $A$ is a square matrix, then $A+{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric.\n- If $A$ is a symmetric matrix, then ${A}^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists).\n- The sum of two $n\\times n$ matrices is always symmetric." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any square matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+{A}^{T})^{T} & ={A}^{T}+({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}+A \\\\  & =A+{A}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any non-singular symmetric matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}^{-1})^{T} & =({A}^{T})^{-1} \\\\  & ={A}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrices \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nHowever, their sum is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275136 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162024 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bcd0ca26-3f22-533f-9d08-1533eeddbc79" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Properties of Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59511ecd-760f-5a26-8413-c806af70d849" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273150} {:answer/id #uuid "74394f47-61a5-59ed-8042-940ea3fed5d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273382} {:answer/id #uuid "b5b1f62c-834f-579c-a300-554956679735" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273963} {:answer/id #uuid "c0528799-16a1-5629-96a3-8f50e1d8f001" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274052} {:answer/id #uuid "dc006e14-42a2-5536-a14c-1676307fd9ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74394f47-61a5-59ed-8042-940ea3fed5d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273382} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d8b66c2-34e1-5bb1-904f-4db1604f1532" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "416a1d82-1004-5007-b882-19d5aaa3af4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-143686" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The product of two $n\\times n$ diagonal matrices is always symmetric.\n- If $A$ is *any* $n\\times n$ matrix, then $A+{A}^{2}$ is always symmetric.\n- If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$ matrix, then $A{A}^{T}-{A}^{T}A$ is always symmetric." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any diagonal matrices $A$ and $B,$ their product $AB$ is also diagonal, and any diagonal matrix is symmetric.\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nHowever, $A+{A}^{2}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+{A}^{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any square matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A{A}^{T}-{A}^{T}A)^{T} & =(A{A}^{T})^{T}-({A}^{T}A)^{T} \\\\  & =({A}^{T})^{T}{A}^{T}-{A}^{T}({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & =A{A}^{T}-{A}^{T}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275137 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162024 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bcd0ca26-3f22-533f-9d08-1533eeddbc79" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Properties of Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15274c66-a01d-5e11-adcf-f5b7af833716" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272577} {:answer/id #uuid "33f93cc6-5c19-5d84-abe2-dc0f4f827464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272849} {:answer/id #uuid "3bfa656f-46fd-5b47-8681-a4125453c988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272905} {:answer/id #uuid "7ec937ca-a0b9-5506-bdd2-a01d7c5c3993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273474} {:answer/id #uuid "c17c205f-6fa2-54a3-af74-d30ca7ac801f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ec937ca-a0b9-5506-bdd2-a01d7c5c3993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273474} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f76ddf6-2fd6-51e1-9516-877b87bf72c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274662}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "62cdcd34-c338-59e0-b65d-2d882ff231e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-143687" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The sum of two $n\\times n$ diagonal matrices is always symmetric.\n- If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$  matrix, then $A-{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric.\n- The product of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any diagonal matrices $A$ and $B,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+B)^{T} & ={A}^{T}+{B}^{T} \\\\  & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nHowever, $A-{A}^{T}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{A}^{T} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nHowever, their product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275138 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162024 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bcd0ca26-3f22-533f-9d08-1533eeddbc79" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Properties of Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "048a1426-e467-5f7e-8f82-a4d34764959c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272427} {:answer/id #uuid "5277c838-1f6f-5a65-99df-70de2b3be811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273088} {:answer/id #uuid "91b72d80-d1b0-5714-90ef-ff9b08666590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273654} {:answer/id #uuid "96acc4c6-843e-5d92-95f4-295d7b6576f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273692} {:answer/id #uuid "cfb2b63c-1f8a-5d48-8978-0439e3ae672a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cfb2b63c-1f8a-5d48-8978-0439e3ae672a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274196} :answer-field/id #uuid "efa0f764-9003-584a-9cb0-87716a826d67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "812925c4-9b82-5457-b638-58a29d609de3" :question/math-academy-id "q-143688" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $A$ is symmetric, then ${A}^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists).\n- The sum of *any* two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric.\n- If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$ matrix, then ${A}^{T}A$ is always symmetric." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any non-singular symmetric matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}^{-1})^{T} & =({A}^{T})^{-1} \\\\  & ={A}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any two symmetric matrices $A$ and $B,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+B)^{T} & ={A}^{T}+{B}^{T} \\\\  & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}^{T}A)^{T} & ={A}^{T}({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162063 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38398622-ca45-5eb6-a589-96aec162960a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Joint Distribution of Two Discrete Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89bddea6-bbe2-51b8-adf4-de306f1c6836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273576} {:answer/id #uuid "8e52a1ab-35d5-5c3f-8591-929097128649" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273617} {:answer/id #uuid "94a7a522-7254-5d79-914a-36556eb0b65c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273682} {:answer/id #uuid "b22de866-0abf-5b09-a709-899483e618d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273926} {:answer/id #uuid "ea17a99a-d10e-5140-b7fb-13d127018d64" :an